{"kind":"artifact","text":"Part one of the kernel admission gaps: rests_on resolvability. internal/kernel now refuses a submission whose rests_on carries a canonical event identifier for this log that names no position in it, with ErrUnresolvedReference naming the reference it could not resolve. A reference that makes no claim about this log - another workroom identifier, a URL, any other opaque string - is carried unchanged, so the kernel gains no application semantics. Resolution reads a commit index maintained beside the dedup index on every path that builds one (cold scan, streamed delta, checkpoint restore, local append), so the check asks Git nothing and a record with 256 bases costs the same Git work as one with a single basis; that choice is deliberate, because the performance contract tracks git_process_count on a dependency fan-out axis up to width 256. The gate is on submission only: history keeps its dangling references and still verifies.","body":{"commit":"9197c43b9fb0fdc0f74e418a9171e362272583f9","conditions":"Admission-time gate only. Verification, checkpoints and continuation are unchanged, and records already sequenced with dangling references still verify and still load. Zero additional Git subprocesses per append. Back-pressure and the over-ceiling attachment path, the other two conditions of the request, are not in this head.","path":"internal/kernel","tests":"go test -count=1 ./... and go test -race -count=1 ./... at this head: every package green except internal/docset TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, which fails byte-identically at origin/main ca2836ab and is untouched by this head; gofmt -l clean; go build ./... and go vet ./... clean; three mutations of the new seam each fail the tests that pin it"}}